#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048245 :topic/math-academy-id 3770 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489 :topic/math-academy-id 478 :topic/title "Integrating Trigonometric Functions Using Substitution"}] :topic/id #uuid "7280f087-059d-5524-8f8b-a1fe79b4ef9d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064245 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064099 :question/id #uuid "60a223c7-3d1c-50d6-be7c-330ba039635c" :question/math-academy-id "e-491" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv' \\, \\textrm{d}x= uv - \\int vu' \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle  \\int xe^{7x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle  \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{7x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}-\\int \\frac{1}{7}{e}^{7x}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}\\int {e}^{7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{{e}^{7x}}{7})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}x{e}^{7x}-\\frac{1}{49}{e}^{7x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing an Exponential Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064246 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064100 :question/id #uuid "804f45cd-2082-5521-b4db-a32169716626" :question/math-academy-id "e-492" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x \\cos{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x= uv- \\int vu'\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x \\cos{2x} \\textrm{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int uv'\\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Indefinite Integral Containing a Trigonometric Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064117 :question/id #uuid "aaeb0407-771b-5dbc-8a50-e7bc0e3477c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6209" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/5} x\\sin(5x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\int (-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /5}+{\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)}{5})|}_{0}^{\\pi /5} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)]+[\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\cdot (-1)+0]+[0-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Definite Integral Using Integration by Parts"}] :topic/math-academy-id 317 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169 :topic/math-academy-id 2096 :topic/title "Inner Product Spaces"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048569 :topic/math-academy-id 877 :topic/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048685 :topic/math-academy-id 3401 :topic/title "Moment-Generating Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049050 :topic/math-academy-id 6372 :topic/title "Laplace Transforms of Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049374 :topic/math-academy-id 3275 :topic/title "Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433 :topic/math-academy-id 3289 :topic/title "The Gamma Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289 :topic/math-academy-id 2107 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050355 :topic/math-academy-id 3187 :topic/title "Solving Systems of ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521 :topic/math-academy-id 1140 :topic/title "Using Integration by Parts to Calculate Integrals With Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050787 :topic/math-academy-id 1043 :topic/title "Calculating Areas Bounded by Parametric Functions"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Integration by Parts"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/math-academy-id 3692 :topic/title "Gradient Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051085 :topic/math-academy-id 2132 :topic/title "The Curl of a Vector Field"}] :topic/id #uuid "76f021c9-2347-5c45-873f-1f9d1e3473c8" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166863 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166717 :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3149 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/math-academy-id 1951 :topic/title "Calculating Potential Functions"}] :topic/title "Conservative Vector Fields in the Cartesian Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136 :topic/math-academy-id 244 :topic/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}] :topic/id #uuid "773e9f74-2606-5a7c-96ea-1dbef24812bb" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059714 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059527 :question/id #uuid "e2b211fe-d37a-55dd-bf76-9ffab2b6f128" :question/math-academy-id "e-4297" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -4, 6 \\rangle$. Find $\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (6)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 + 36} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059528 :question/id #uuid "d4ad6011-24a4-5642-997f-dcc4e807224f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4298" :question/problem "Let $A(1,3)$ and $B(2,-2)$. Find $\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/f9/f95ee3c5c81750d97019d910350e4c78f0962baa682c834928450a451691145a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\nOA^(→) = a = i + 3j,; OB^(→) = b = 2i - 2j\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= OB^(→) - OA^(→) \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid AB^(→) \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector From Position Vectors and then Computing its Magnitude"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059530 :question/id #uuid "65198cdd-dd3b-5840-aaf3-cecd96eb9d3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4301" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + 2b}{\\mid a + 2b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + 2b &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 + 2 \\\\ 0 + (-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + 2b}{\\mid a + 2b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(a + 2b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :topic/math-academy-id 176 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050270 :topic/math-academy-id 2036 :topic/title "Velocity and Acceleration for Plane Motion"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050938 :topic/math-academy-id 243 :topic/title "Calculating the Dot Product Using Angle and Magnitude"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051021 :topic/math-academy-id 1277 :topic/title "Calculating the Magnitude of Cartesian Vectors in 3D"}] :topic/title "Calculating the Magnitude of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051000 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048512 :topic/math-academy-id 3699 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Line Segments"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050616 :topic/math-academy-id 3690 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Ellipses"}] :topic/id #uuid "789238e2-4251-5080-bbc3-e713bd84a64b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "bbd835f9-a428-558c-9db7-838d02267a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9512" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5c788ad2c220b70a7f1d333680a70933f9b490a0db11fb3440dd3a2e06fa01.png)\n\nFind a definite integral that's equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C (x+y) \\, \\textrm{d} x,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}x  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,0)$ and $(3,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0+t(3-0),\\,0+t(3-0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3t+3t \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6t\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to X"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513" :question/problem "![](images/74/74033bfe17cefe62d5306b2186b9816ba26b816ece10c5dab5178abe1a9aaf89.png)\n\nFind the definite integral equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_C x^2 \\, \\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $25x^2 + 4y^2 = 100,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 5\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc3a36829c9780c34e13f253d607c3849bfd3ef1399a77a79a3dd3d30ef7140.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2109 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666 :topic/math-academy-id 3705 :topic/title "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687 :topic/math-academy-id 3708 :topic/title "Properties of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :topic/title "Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}]}
